# The untwisting limit and the CG generating function {#sec:untwist}

**Punchline.** In the regime $\zeta=\epsilon\phi$, $\omega=\epsilon\hat\omega$ the jet formula is a statement about the $\epsilon$-jet of the Q-functions, with cancellations of depth up to six orders. Reduced to Laurent data it becomes a polynomial identity in $(\hat\omega_1,\hat\omega_1')$ whose solution is the CG generating function. For all six HW branches of the adjoint the identity holds to $10^{-55}$--$10^{-157}$ and returns every CG coefficient, $\phi$-independent to $2\cdot10^{-77}$.

## The regime and the difficulty {#sec:regime}

Let $\zeta=\epsilon\phi$, $\omega_1=\epsilon\hat\omega_1$, $\omega_1'=\epsilon\hat\omega_1'$ and $\epsilon\to0$. Chain side (Section~\ref{sec:chain}): $\langle\Phi_\infty|\Psi_B\rangle=\langle\varphi_u\otimes e_1^T|\Psi_0\rangle(1+O(\epsilon))$ for every HW-limit branch $\Psi_B=\Psi_0+\epsilon\Psi_1+\cdots$ with $\Psi_0$ an exact HW vector of weight $\mu$, G-norm 1; so
$$
\langle\varphi_u(\hat\omega_1,\hat\omega_1')\otimes e_1|\Psi_0\rangle=\sum_\nu\bigl[e_1^T\rho(n')\bigr]_\nu\,C_\nu,\qquad C_\nu=\langle e_\nu\otimes e_1|\Psi_0\rangle,\label{eq:CGgf}
$$
is the CG generating function: a polynomial in the rotation parameters whose coefficients are the CG coefficients of the GT basis. We want to write the left-hand side as a Q-expression using (\ref{eq:jet}).

The difficulty is that the limit is delicate on the Q side. At $\zeta=0$ the three dual Q-functions $e^{\zeta_ju}p_j$ lose their exponentials and their Wronskians vanish (they carry factors $\zeta_b-\zeta_a$), so the untwisted limits of the three branch Q's are linearly dependent: the limit of the solution space is a $\zeta$-jet ($p^{(0)}$ and its $\epsilon$-derivatives), not just a triple of polynomials. Moreover the jet formula's total is $\epsilon^{N_B}$ smaller than its individual terms, with $N_B=8,7,7,6,6,4$ for $(4,2,0),(4,1,1),(3,3,0),(3,2,1)\times2,(2,2,2)$, while single terms are $O(\epsilon^2\ldots\epsilon^3)$: a cancellation of up to six orders.

## $\epsilon$-order bookkeeping {#sec:bookkeeping}

The ingredients scale as follows (for the adjoint, at generic $\phi$; $\phi'$ gives identical exponents). The frame scalar is $c=\epsilon^8\hat c$ with $\hat c=(\phi_2-\hat\omega_1)(\phi_3-\hat\omega_1)^2(\phi_2-\hat\omega_1')(\phi_3-\hat\omega_1')^2(\phi_1-\phi_2)^2$; the kernel splits by degree in $(\omega_1,\omega_1')$, $G^s(\epsilon\hat\omega)=\sum_d\epsilon^dG^{s[d]}(\hat\omega)$ (at fixed $\theta_{12}$); $M_1M_2\sim\epsilon^2$ on all branches; $\langle\Phi_\infty|\Psi_B\rangle\sim\epsilon^0$ at all 16 grid points; and
$$
\langle x_0^G|\Psi_B\rangle\sim\epsilon^{2+k_B},\qquad k_B=0,1,1,2,2,4\quad\text{for}\quad(4,2,0),\,(4,1,1),\,(3,3,0),\,(3,2,1)\times2,\,(2,2,2).\label{eq:kB}
$$
Table~\ref{tab:order} gives the exponents. The interpretation of $k_B$: on the exact HW kets of every non-top $\mu$, $\langle x(\phi)\otimes x(\phi)|v\rangle=0$ identically ($\le4\cdot10^{-202}$ at 200 digits, both $\phi$, the whole HW space and both octet copies); for the top it is $(\phi_2-\phi_1)^2$. So $\langle x_0^G|\Psi_B\rangle$ gets its first contribution from the $k_B$-th order vector $\Psi_{k_B}$, and we define
$$
X_B:=\langle x(\phi)\otimes x(\phi)|\Psi_{k_B}\rangle,\label{eq:XB}
$$
which turns out to be a product of differences $\phi_i-\phi_j$ (below). The consistency check is $N_B=8+0-(2+k_B)+2=8-k_B$ on all six branches.

