## The leading polynomial identity {#sec:leadpoly}

All orders $t<N_B$ of $\sum_{ij}G^s_{ij}(\epsilon\hat\omega)N_iN_j$ cancel, with residuals $4.5\cdot10^{-51}$ ($(4,2,0)$), $1.2\cdot10^{-55}$, $1.1\cdot10^{-55}$, $6.6\cdot10^{-66}$, $2.9\cdot10^{-84}$ at $t=N_B-1$, against $0.2$--$0.4$ at $t=N_B$. The leading polynomial is $L_B(\hat\omega)=\sum_{d+n+m=N_B}G^{s[d]}_{ij}N^{(n)}_iN^{(m)}_j$, where $N_i^{(n)}$ is the $\epsilon^n$ coefficient of $N_i$, and the identity is
$$
L_B(\hat\omega_1,\hat\omega_1')=\hat c\;\langle\varphi_u\otimes e_1|\Psi_0\rangle\;\frac{m_B}{X_B},\qquad m_B=\lim\epsilon^{-2}M_1M_2,\ \ \kappa_B=1,\label{eq:leadpoly}
$$
as a polynomial in $(\hat\omega_1,\hat\omega_1')$, coefficient by coefficient (relative coefficient residual $10^{-39}$ to $10^{-75}$ over six branches and two directions; the constant $\kappa_B$ between the two sides is exactly 1). Each octet copy pairs with its own HW ket (different generating functions, same constant $R_B=m_B/X_B$). For the top state $m_B=-(\phi_3-\phi_2)^2\theta_{12}^2$ ($Q_1=1$; checked to 13 digits), so $R_B(\mathrm{top})=-(\phi_3-\phi_2)^2\theta_{12}^2/(\phi_2-\phi_1)^2$; in general $M_s=P^3A_sQ_1$ with $A_s=(\zeta_3-\zeta_2)e^{\sigma\theta_s}(\theta_s-\theta_t)$ and $m_B=-(\phi_3-\phi_2)^2\theta_{12}^2Q_1^0(\theta_1)Q_1^0(\theta_2)$ (not separately checked beyond the top). Which $(d,n,m)$ contribute at order $N_B$ (terms above $10^{-30}$): $(4,2,0)$: $d=2..6$, $n,m=1..4$ (largest $(4,2,2),(3,2,3),(5,1,2),(4,1,3)$); $(4,1,1)$ and $(3,3,0)$: $d=1..5$, $n,m\le5$ (largest $(3,2,2),(4,1,2),(2,2,3)$); octets $d=0..4$, $n,m\le4$; singlet only six triples $(1,1,2),(2,1,1),(1,2,1),(0,2,2),(0,1,3),(0,3,1)$.

